<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Confederation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1836">1836</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Confederation was fought from 1836 to 1839 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, with Peruvian exiles and Argentine support, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>–Bolivian Confederation created by the Bolivian president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a>. After a failed first Chilean expedition, the Chilean general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-bulnes">Manuel Bulnes</a> defeated Santa Cruz at Yungay in January 1839. The confederation was dissolved and Peru and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> separated again.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-confederation.jpg" alt="War of the Confederation" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Yungay.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The confederation created by Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a> in 1836 alarmed its neighbours. In Chile Diego Portales, the power behind President José Joaquín Prieto, feared it would upset the balance of power and threaten Chilean independence, and the rivalry between the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callao">Callao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valparaiso">Valparaíso</a> sharpened the quarrel. A tariff war followed, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> raising its duty on Chilean wheat and Chile its duty on Peruvian sugar. In 1836 the exiled former president Ramón Freire sailed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> with Confederate help to overthrow Prieto and was captured, and on the night of 21 August 1836 a Chilean raid under Victorino Garrido seized three Confederate ships at Callao.</p><p>Santa Cruz accepted every Chilean demand except the dissolution of the confederation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> declared war on 28 December 1836. The Argentine Confederation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-de-rosas">Juan Manuel de Rosas</a> declared war on 9 May 1837, but the two allies fought separately throughout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 6 June 1837 Portales was shot by mutinous officers at Quillota, a murder blamed on Santa Cruz that rallied Chilean opinion behind the war. A first expedition of about 2,800 men under Admiral Manuel Blanco Encalada landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islay">Islay</a> and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>, but Santa Cruz surrounded it, and on 17 November 1837 Blanco Encalada signed the Treaty of Paucarpata, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> repudiated. In 1838 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-bulnes">Manuel Bulnes</a> led a second army, which won at Portada de Guías outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 21 August. On 12 January 1839 the Chilean fleet defeated the Confederate squadron at Casma, and at Yungay on 20 January the Confederates lost over 2,400 casualties and more than 1,600 prisoners, against about 1,300 Chilean dead.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Santa Cruz had already been overthrown as president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> by General José Miguel de Velasco, and he fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecuador">Ecuador</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. On 25 August 1839 General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a>, president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, declared the confederation dissolved, and the Chilean troops left on 19 October. Gamarra then invaded Bolivia himself and was killed at the Battle of Ingaví on 18 November 1841; peace came in 1842. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> the army tripled in size during the war, and historians see in it the consolidation of Peruvian and Chilean ideas of nationality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Confederation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
